FAQ = [
 ("What is a dynamic roof load limit?",
  "It is the most weight the roof can carry while the vehicle is moving, and it is the figure that governs a roof rack, a cargo box, bikes or a kayak. It counts everything above the roof: the crossbars or platform, the carrier and the cargo inside. Subaru states it that way for the Forester, calling 176 lb the dynamic capacity and adding a separate 700 lb static figure for a parked car. A static limit only matters when the vehicle is stopped, which is why it comes up with rooftop tents. If a maker prints one number without a label, as Kia and Hyundai do, the safe reading is that it is the driving limit."),
 ("Does the roof load limit include the crossbars and the rack?",
  "Yes. Every maker figure the site has read is written as a total for the roof or the rails, and the bars, feet, platform and carrier all sit on that roof. Honda words the Pilot figure as up to 165 lb of cargo carried on the rails using accessory crossbars, and the site's guides treat the bars as part of that budget. The practical effect is large. A Front Runner 3/4 platform for the 2010–2024 4Runner weighs about 59 lb, and the site's guide notes that leaves roughly 60 lb for gear under the 120 lb figure it uses. A 40 to 57 lb cargo box takes a similar bite out of a 165 lb roof."),
 ("Why does my crossbar say 300 lb when the roof is rated 165 lb?",
  "Because the bar rating describes the bar, not the vehicle. Amazon crossbar titles in the site's guides print 220, 260, 300 and 330 lb, and the brand-name systems from Thule, Yakima, Rhino-Rack and Malone are mostly rated at 165 lb. None of those numbers changes what the roof structure and the rail mounts can take. The rule in every site guide is the same: the lower of the vehicle's figure and the rack's figure is the limit, and the rack's own weight comes out of it. A stiffer bar flexes less under a loaded box. It does not make the roof stronger."),
 ("Which vehicles in the chart have a roof figure from the maker?",
  "Five generations carry a figure from a maker document: the 2020–2025 Subaru Outback (150 lb standard rails; 200 lb dynamic and 700 lb static on the Wilderness, from Subaru trim sheets), the 2019–2024 Subaru Forester (176 lb; 220 lb and 800 lb on the Wilderness, from Subaru sheets and a 2022 release), the 2020–2025 Kia Telluride (220 lb, 2024 owner's manual), the 2020–2025 Hyundai Palisade (220 lb roof rails load capacity, spec sheets) and the 2023–2026 Honda Pilot (165 lb on the rails with accessory crossbars, Honda Info Center). Tesla publishes ratings for its own racks on the Model 3 and Model Y, but its manuals print no roof figure."),
 ("What is the roof load limit on a Toyota 4Runner?",
  "For the 2010–2024 truck the site uses 120 lb, and it is not confirmed. The figure comes from a reader comment on Trail4Runner citing a 2016 SR5 owner's manual, and the comment does not say whether it is the rail limit or the roof limit. A Toyota dealer's parts page for the Genuine Toyota cross bar kit PT278-89170 prints 132 lb spread across both bars, which is a bar rating with no model years listed. For the 2025–2026 truck the site stores no figure. GearJunkie's first-drive review reports 165 lb dynamic and 770 lb static, and one owner on 4Runner6G cites 125 lb for the factory crossbars. The owner's manual is the authority for both."),
 ("What is the RAV4 roof load limit?",
  "The site's vehicle data stores 165 lb for the 2019–2025 RAV4, and the site's RAV4 roof rack and cargo box guides say plainly that the number could not be traced to a Toyota document. RAV4Resource lists 176.4 lb (80 kg) for 2019–2024 models and points readers to the Cargo and Luggage section of the owner's manual. A Toyota dealer catalog page for Toyota's own Adventure cross bars prints no load figure and says to see the owner's manual. So the chart carries 165 lb as the site's working figure, marked not confirmed, with 176.4 lb beside it. Read your manual before loading bars and a box."),
 ("What happens on trucks with a bare cab roof or a removable top?",
  "The chart has no roof figure for any of the 14 pickup generations the site covers, and the site has no roof rack guide for them. Their roof_type is stored as bare, or removable on the Gladiator, and roof_load_lb is empty, so each row reads not published / not confirmed. That is not a claim that the cab cannot carry a load. It means the site routes truck cargo to the bed: bed racks, tonneau covers and the bed's own payload. If you want bars on a pickup cab, the owner's manual is the only source the site can point to, and cab-roof clamp kits from Thule and Yakima carry their own ratings that still sit under the manual's figure."),
 ("Can I put a rooftop tent on an SUV with a 165 lb roof limit?",
  "Only if the tent plus the rack stays under the dynamic figure while you drive, and that is where most plans fail. A hard-shell tent alone can weigh 100 to 150 lb; the site's Bronco guide notes one owner quoting a Yakima tent at 101.4 lb. On the road that tent and its bars have to fit inside the driving limit, and once parked the static limit covers the tent and the people in it. Subaru publishes static figures for the Outback Wilderness (700 lb) and the Forester (700 lb, 800 lb Wilderness), and Tesla publishes 495 lb for its Model 3 rack. Most makers in the chart publish no static figure at all, so ask the tent maker and read the manual."),
 ("Do Thule and Yakima ratings override the vehicle figure?",
  "No. Thule and Yakima publish 165 lb for most of the complete systems in the site's guides, and etrailer adds a note on some listings to follow the vehicle's own roof limit. The two numbers work together: the vehicle's figure caps the total, the rack's figure caps what that rack can hold, and the lower one applies. On the Telluride the roof is rated 220 lb by Kia and the Thule system at 165 lb, so 165 lb wins. On the Bronco the owner-quoted 110 lb is far below the 200 to 800 lb rack ratings, so 110 lb wins. One more wrinkle from the site's Tahoe guide: Thule rates the flush-rail feet at 165 lb and the bars at 220 lb, and the feet decide."),
 ("Where do I find the roof load limit in my owner's manual?",
  "Look in the loading or cargo chapter, under a heading like Roof Rack, Roof Luggage Rack, Roof Rails or Cargo and Luggage. Kia's 2024 Telluride manual has a Roof Rack page that prints 220 lb evenly distributed. Jeep's 2023 Grand Cherokee manual lists a Roof Luggage Rack section at page 79 of the PDF. Tesla's Model 3 and Model Y manuals cover roof racks on their Vehicle Loading page, and print no weight. If the page gives a kilogram figure, multiply by 2.2: 75 kg is 165 lb, 80 kg is 176 lb, 100 kg is 220 lb. Those three conversions explain most of the numbers in the chart."),
 ("Why do so many figures land on exactly 165 lb?",
  "Because 165 lb is 75 kg, and 75 kg is the standard rating Thule, Yakima and many other rack makers print for a complete crossbar system. Honda uses it for the Pilot's rails, Toyota's own crossbars for the Highlander are listed at 75 kg by a Canadian Toyota dealer, and Tesla rates its Model Y rack at 165 lb. When a vehicle has no published roof figure, the site's guides fall back on the 165 lb kit rating as the working ceiling and say so. That is why the chart separates the roof figure from what the figure covers: on the Highlander, Tahoe, Explorer and Grand Cherokee, 165 lb is a rack number, not a roof number."),
]

